  "query_preview": "..(Wednesday through Monday)\nIssued at 445 PM EDT MON AUG 31 2026\n\nThe long wave pattern will feature amplified flow across the \nmajority of the CONUS through early next week. Troughing will \ngenerally dominate west of the Rockies. A more anomalous ridge, \ninitially sprawled from Kentucky through the Carolinas into  \nThursday, will temporarily dampen and retreat westward by early \nthis weekend. The ridge center will then strengthen once again as \nit anchors over the southern Plains into early next week. This \nwill allow for the Ohio and Tennessee valleys to be subject to \nnorthwesterly flow aloft, with bouts of traversing short wave \nenergy surfing southeast across the region. While model agreement \nis solid through Thursday, detail differences bring more spread \nregarding weather specifics by this weekend and into early next \nweekend.\n\nConfidence remains high for the hot temperatures to continue across \neastern Kentucky through the end of the work week. Highs will be \nin the mid 90s for most locations both Wednesday and Thursday, \nperhaps flirting with a few daily records on Wednesday. As the \nupper level ridge centers overhead of the Commonwealth, expect the\nassociated subsidence to keep a lid on convection. The ridge will\nlose its grip by Friday, with a surface cold front approaching \nfrom the north. Rain chances will return across the area, with one\nmore hot and humid day expected for Friday, as temperatures \nreturn to the lower 90s, coupled with dew points in the low to mid\n70s. Low level northwesterly to northeasterly flow moves in this \nweekend, putting an end to the 90 degree days for the entire area\nby Sunday, with highs returning to more seasonable 80s for early \nSeptember. While PoPs look to lower into early next week, given\nthe lack of surface features, the lingering northwest flow aloft\nprecludes going completely dry for any given period at this time.",
